---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import {DESIGN_URL, GITHUB_URL} from "@/data/env";
import {PANEL_ACCENT} from "@brewdocs.beer/design";
---
<Layout title="For developers">
    <main
        class="min-h-screen w-screen flex flex-col items-center px-6 pt-24 pb-16 lg:pt-28"
        style="background-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-200) 85%, transparent) 65%, var(--color-base-300) 100%);">
        <h1 class="mt-5 text-3xl lg:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">For developers</h1>

        <div class="mt-8 lg:max-w-screen-md text-left flex flex-col gap-4">
            <p class="px-5">
                BrewDocs isn't only for a brewing audience. It's also built by a software
                engineer, in the open, and a fair bit of what went into it is worth a look on
                its own — so this page is for the other engineers, and how the thing is put
                together.
            </p>

            <div class={PANEL_ACCENT}>
                <div class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-primary">Design system</div>
                <p class="mt-2 text-base">
                    Almost all of it is generated and maintained by Claude Code "agents". That
                    works because of the investment in fundamental design patterns underneath:
                    DaisyUI as the foundation, an extended token set on top of it, and a dash
                    of <code>CLAUDE.md</code> guidance to keep the agents
                    pointed at the right one. It doubles as a quick reference if you just want
                    to see what's available.
                </p>
                <div class="mt-4">
                    <a href={DESIGN_URL} class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">View the Storybook</a>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class={PANEL_ACCENT}>
                <div class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-primary">Agentic engineering</div>
                <p class="mt-2 text-base">
                    The agentic setup is worth a look too — how it got to where it is, and how it
                    ended up modularized as "Claude Team", which now gets reused across my other
                    projects rather than living here.
                </p>
                <div class="mt-4">
                    <a href="/claude" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Read about BrewDocs: Claude Team</a>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="px-5">
                <div class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-primary">GitHub</div>
                <p class="mt-2 text-base">
                    Or start at the top: the BrewDocs repo is public, and everything above is in
                    it — the app, this site, the design system and the knowledge base.
                </p>
                <div class="mt-4">
                    <a href={GITHUB_URL} class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">View the Repository</a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>
</Layout>
